f 1061 12596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055520 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "219dccb2-c3f8-5ef8-af33-9ea8f7e5c0f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055520 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1061 625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac48d370-ba29-5343-bf37-4b82f3611773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1061 626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea42d926-8045-5d22-9aa7-2fd3a3f233c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055845 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1061 629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8893f4d9-c611-591e-80a2-799835854aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055846 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1061 631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41c0eff0-f259-5568-b570-bafddf204af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1061 633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "421f20ea-c000-57af-973a-3496371dd497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055849 #edb/ref 1061 634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055849 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "814ed39b-61cd-5eb5-92ed-72ff4dbe9a1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055849 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1064 "For a function $f(x)$, we can express the derivative as $f'(x)$.\n\nRecall that we can also express the derivative using the **differential operator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the differential operator while computing the derivative, too. For example, the power rule can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left(x^n \\right) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\big(\\cdots\\big)\n$$\n\njust means that we need to differentiate whatever is inside $\\big(\\cdots\\big)$ with respect to $x$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1065 2647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "537fb074-19a0-542c-9a84-c4542e26b33f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055962 #edb/ref 1067 "The Differential Operator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055978 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nusing the definition of the derivative. However, there is a trick to do it faster!\n\nThe trick is called the **power rule**, and it states that for a general power function in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthe derivative is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nThe function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\ntakes the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^n\n$$\n\nwith $n=2$, so we can use the power rule to compute its derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= nx^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2 - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{1} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result agrees with what we would get if we used the definition of the derivative. However, using the power rule, the computations are much easier!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055978 #edb/ref 1065 337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055978 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "37492dee-669b-5eb2-9676-b78afaf6acb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055978 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055983 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, if\n\n$$\nf(x) = k\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant, then $f'(x) = 0$. There are a couple ways to see why this is.\n\nFirst, let's take the constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\n$$\n\nbut write it a little differently:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2x^0\n$$\n\nTo differentiate the function above, we can use the power rule with $n=0$. Applying the power rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 0 \\cdot 2x^{0 - 1} \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 2x^{-1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere's nothing special about the number $2$. The same result would hold for *any* constant function.\n\nWe can also think about this geometrically. The function $y=f(x)=2$ is a horizontal line, and it does not rise or fall as we change $x$. So, its slope is zero everywhere, and therefore $f'(x) = 0$ everywhere.\n\n![](images/22/222febb3babc289ba15ba1e187aa9848ea6f2d802bf21ad8e1396b092370d564.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055983 #edb/ref 1065 341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055983 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aba9943d-6c1b-5f0d-b9e0-a0c854c231b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055983 #edb/ref 1067 "The Derivative of the Constant Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1045 "The Power Rule for Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186519665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518981 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8d6616d8-4bd9-5180-836d-7a649de8f37f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519030 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519030 #edb/re